The heat‑transfer resistance of the steel pipe (cylindrical wall)  

\[
R_{pipe}= \frac{\ln (r_o/r_i)}{2\pi k_{steel}} 
= \frac{\ln (6.5/6)}{2\pi (26)}=5.34\times10^{-4}\;{\rm hr\;ft^\circ\!F}
\]

The resistance of the insulation layer  

\[
R_{ins}= \frac{\ln (r_{o,ins}/r_{i,ins})}{2\pi k_{ins}}
= \frac{\ln [(6.5+3.5)/6.5]}{2\pi (0.04)}=2.21\times10^{-2}\;{\rm hr\;ft^\circ\!F}
\]

Total resistance  

\[
R_{tot}=R_{pipe}+R_{ins}=2.27\times10^{-2}\;{\rm hr\;ft^\circ\!F}
\]

The overall temperature difference is \(500-150 = 350^{\circ}\!F\).

Heat loss per foot:

\[
\dot Q = \frac{\Delta T}{R_{tot}}
        = \frac{350}{2.27\times10^{-2}}
        \approx 1.40\times10^{2}\;{\rm (energy/hr·ft)}
\]

So the rate is about **140** per foot of pipe.

The temperature at the pipe‑insulation interface follows the temperature drop across the pipe wall alone:

\[
\Delta T_{pipe}= \dot Q \, R_{pipe}
               = 140 \times 5.34\times10^{-4}
               \approx 0.075^{\circ}\!F
\]

\[
T_{interface}= T_{inner} - \Delta T_{pipe}
             = 500 - 0.075 \approx 496.9^{\circ}\!F
\]

Hence the correct pair is:

**140.28 … per foot, 496.88 °F** (choice C).<|return|>

