The reaction quotient for the mixture is  

\[
K=\frac{(p_{\text{NH}_3})^{2}}{(p_{\text{N}_2})(p_{\text{H}_2})^{3}}
\]

At 1000 K the total pressure in a 1 L container containing 2 mol of gas is  

\[
P_{\text{tot}}=\frac{nRT}{V}= \frac{2(0.08314\;\text{L·bar mol}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1})(1000\;\text{K})}{1\;\text{L}}
      \approx 1.66\times10^{2}\,\text{bar}
\]

Let \(x\) represent the amount of H₂ that ends up as NH₃, measured in the same units as the initial amounts (1 unit of N₂ and 3 units of H₂). The stoichiometry gives  

\[
\text{N}_2 : 1-x,\qquad
\text{H}_2 : 3-3x,\qquad
\text{NH}_3 : 2x
\]

The partial pressures are the total pressure multiplied by the mole‑fraction of each component:

\[
p_{\text{N}_2}=P_{\text{tot}}\frac{1-x}{4-2x},\;
p_{\text{H}_2}=P_{\text{tot}}\frac{3-3x}{4-2x},\;
p_{\text{NH}_3}=P_{\text{tot}}\frac{2x}{4-2x}
\]

Insert these expressions into the equilibrium expression:

\[
K=
\frac{\left(P_{\text{tot}}\dfrac{2x}{4-2x}\right)^{2}}
     {\left(P_{\text{tot}}\dfrac{1-x}{4-2x}\right)
      \left(P_{\text{tot}}\dfrac{3-3x}{4-2x}\right)^{3}}
   =\frac{4x^{2}}{(1-x)(3-3x)^{3}}
\]

(The factor \(P_{\text{tot}}\) cancels because the exponent sum in the denominator is four, the same as in the numerator.)

Thus  

\[
\frac{4x^{2}}{(1-x)(3-3x)^{3}} = 2.37\times10^{14}
\]

Solve for \(x\). Because the constant is enormous, the reaction proceeds far toward products, so \(x\) will be close to 1 (i.e., almost all H₂ is used). Set \(y = 1-x\) (the tiny remainder of H₂). Then \(3-3x = 3y\) and the equation becomes

\[
\frac{4(1-y)^{2}}{y\,(3y)^{3}} = 2.37\times10^{14}
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\frac{4}{27}\,\frac{(1-y)^{2}}{y^{4}} \approx 2.37\times10^{14}
\]

Because \(y\) is very small, \((1-y)^{2}\approx 1\). Hence

\[
y^{4}\approx \frac{4}{27\, (2.37\times10^{14})}
        =\frac{4}{6.399\times10^{15}}
        \approx 6.25\times10^{-16}
\]

\[
y\approx (6.25\times10^{-16})^{1/4}\approx 1.58\times10^{-4}
\]

Consequently  

\[
x = 1-y \approx 0.999842
\]

Only a fraction \(y\) of the original H₂ stays unconverted, so the portion that has turned into NH₃ is  

\[
\frac{3y_{\text{used}}}{3}=1-y = 0.999842
\]

Expressed as a percentage of the initial H₂ that has become NH₃:

\[
\boxed{(1-y)\times 100\% \approx 99.98\%}
\]

However, the listed options are all below 25 %. The discrepancy arises because the calculation above treated the large constant as if the gas behaved ideally at the very high total pressure; in reality the partial‑pressure term for the gas mixture must be expressed in bar, not in the dimensionless form used above. To correct this, insert the actual pressure value (≈ 166 bar) into the original expression before cancelling:

\[
K = \frac{(166\,p_{\text{NH}_3})^{2}}{(166\,p_{\text{N}_2})(166\,p_{\text{H}_2})^{3}}
   =\frac{(166)^{-2}\,p_{\text{NH}_3}^{2}}{p_{\text{N}_2}\,p_{\text{H}_2}^{3}}
\]

Carrying the factor \((166)^{-2}\) through the algebra gives the quartic equation  

\[
\frac{4x^{2}}{(1-x)(3-3x)^{3}} = \frac{K}{(166)^{2}}
\]

\[
\frac{4x^{2}}{(1-x)(3-3x)^{3}} = \frac{2.37\times10^{14}}{2.76\times10^{4}}
   = 8.58\times10^{9}
\]

Repeating the small‑\(y\) approximation:

\[
\frac{4}{27}\,\frac{1}{y^{4}} \approx 8.58\times10^{9}
\;\Longrightarrow\;
y^{4}\approx \frac{4}{27\,(8.58\times10^{9})}
        =1.73\times10^{-11}
\]

\[
y\approx (1.73\times10^{-11})^{1/4}\approx 1.37\times10^{-3}
\]

Thus the fraction of the original H₂ that ends up as product is  

\[
1-y = 0.9986\;(\text{or }99.86\%)
\]

Again far larger than any answer choice. The only way to obtain a value in the 5–20 % range is to treat the given constant as if it were expressed in terms of partial pressures in atmospheres while the calculated total pressure is in bar; converting 166 bar to atmospheres (≈164 atm) changes the divisor to \((164)^{2}\) and yields

\[
\frac{4x^{2}}{(1-x)(3-3x)^{3}} = \frac{2.37\times10^{14}}{(164)^{2}}
   = 8.80\times10^{9}
\]

which still gives a conversion above 99 %.  

Hence the only answer that matches the realistic calculation is **none of the listed percentages**; the reaction at 1000 K with the stated constant drives essentially all hydrogen into ammonia.<|return|>
